        "id": 5255
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-09T16:16:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "How does this compare to just:\n\n```\nstatic SkMutex mutex;\n```\n\n, which seems simpler, and might call the destructor? Do we not want to destruct it at teardown?",
      "revId": "46a8075dcc9f0492dd6aa4497887bf3ac7701ef4",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1dae00c4_da661612",
        "filename": "src/codec/SkCodec.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 175,
      "author": {
        "id": 5255
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-09T16:16:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Some Android clients are very interested in decoding simultaneously from different threads, which this won\u0027t allow. I can think of a couple of ways around this.\n\n- Refcount the `Decoder`, so that once we\u0027ve picked one (i.e. `isFormat` returned `true`), we can release the mutex and call `makeFromStream` without the mutex locked (without fearing that a new one will replace it).\n- Have a method that finalizes the list, so that in the future no new decoders can be added. (There could be other variations on this, like only allowing additions during a callback.) Then you don\u0027t need to worry about mutex contention. There\u0027s a potential downside to this - what if a client wants to add different decoders at different times? I don\u0027t know whether this is a realistic use-case, though. (Certainly Android and Chrome don\u0027t want to do this.) Another way to enable this - they own the list (`vector`), meaning that they could use different lists in different contexts.",
      "revId": "46a8075dcc9f0492dd6aa4497887bf3ac7701ef4",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
